.TH LONGWIRE-PROFILES 5 "November 2026" "longwire 0.5.40" "longwire manual"
.SH NAME
longwire-profiles \- saved longwire connections
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/longwire/profiles
.SH DESCRIPTION
A profile is a saved connection: which switchboard, the token it asked for, how to attach, and so on.
The file holds all of them.
.BR longwire (1),
the Control Panel pane and the menu bar item all read it, so a setting is made in one place.
.PP
Because it holds tokens, the directory is mode 0700 and the file mode 0600.
A token is an invitation to register once, not an identity; the identity (the seed) is kept elsewhere and never enters this file.
Anyone who can read your home directory can read your tokens.
.PP
The file can be edited by hand.
.B longwire profile
rewrites it whole, so a comment you add is not kept.
.SH FORMAT
A line
.B default
.I name
names the default profile.
A line
.B profile
.I name
starts a profile; the indented lines that follow belong to it.
Each is
.IB key " = " value
and the value runs to the end of the line.
A profile name is letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens.
.PP
.nf
.RS
default home

profile home
  switchboard = vps.example.org:6741/lw1...
  token = 5fe1c0ab...
  attach = jack
  interface = lw0
  address = 10.77.0.2/24
  keep_jack = yes
  name = osakka
  listed = no
  answer = known
.RE
.fi
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B switchboard
.IR HOST:PORT/KEY :
the switchboard's address, a slash, and its full address
.RB ( lw1... )
or 64 hex digits.
.TP
.B token
The registration token, for a switchboard that wants one.
Needed the first time a machine registers; after that the switchboard knows its key and the token can be removed.
.TP
.B attach
.B jack
(a tap device for one machine) or
.B splice
(a NIC, for a whole site).
.TP
.B interface
The tap device, called
.B lw0
to
.B lw99
(default
.BR lw0 ),
or the NIC to splice.
.TP
.B address
For a jack: this machine's address on the line, like 10.77.0.2/24, put on the device for you when the node is turned on.
Every machine on a line needs its own, in one subnet.
The word
.B dhcp
instead asks a DHCP server on the line, through a client (dhclient, dhcpcd or udhcpc) that the same step starts; it takes the address and the network route only, never a default route or name servers.
Empty leaves the device alone.
.TP
.B gateway
For a jack: a router on the line that reaches other networks, an address inside the jack's own network (not its first or last address, nor this machine's).
Used only with
.B routes
or
.BR everything ,
and needs a static
.BR address .
Without one,
.B everything
goes out through the member that shares its internet.
.TP
.B routes
Networks to reach through the gateway, like 192.168.150.0/24, separated by commas (at most 16).
Never the default route, nothing wider than a /8, nothing on the line itself; put on with the device, and gone with it.
Needs a static
.BR address .
Without a
.B gateway
they are left out.
.TP
.B everything
.B yes
sends all of this machine's traffic through the line, from a routing table of the jack's own; the machine's own default route is never changed, and is used again the moment the node stops.
With a
.B gateway
(which then needs a static
.BR address ),
it goes to that router.
With no
.BR gateway ,
it goes out through the member of the line that shares its internet (see
.BR exit ):
nothing is typed but this, and an
.B address
of the jack's own, fixed or
.BR dhcp .
While no member shares, nothing goes out (it does not leak to the local network).
For
.B "attach = jack"
only; not with
.BR exit .
It fails open: when the node stops, traffic goes the machine's own way again, unencrypted.
.TP
.B exit_through
With
.B everything
and no
.BR gateway :
the number of the member to go out through, like 447\-120\-933, when more than one shares its internet.
It must be sharing, or nothing goes out.
Empty: the only one, or the lowest number.
.TP
.B exit
.B yes
shares this machine's internet with the line: the members that send everything through the line with no gateway go out through this machine, and the internet sees its address.
The jack helper turns forwarding on and adds an nftables table of the jack's own that lets only the line's network out, masqueraded, lets only the answers back in, and keeps the line off the machine's other networks; all of it is undone exactly when sharing stops or the jack goes.
Needs a fixed
.B address
(not
.BR dhcp )
and nftables.
For
.B "attach = jack"
only; not with
.BR everything .
This machine sees everything its users send outside the line, as their internet provider would: share only from a machine the line trusts that much.
.B longwire exit on
sets it.
.TP
.B dns
With
.BR everything :
a name server asked through the line for every name (a router on the line, or, out through a member that shares its internet, any resolver such as 1.1.1.1), set on the jack in systemd\-resolved; passed over, like the routes, whenever no node holds the jack.
Without systemd\-resolved, names stay with the machine's own name server.
.TP
.B keep_jack
.BR yes " (the default) or " no .
With yes, turning the node off leaves the jack, so the next time needs no password; with no, it removes the jack too.
.B longwire off
reads it from the profile the node was turned on with.
.TP
.B name
The name announced to the nodes you call.
A claim, never proof.
.TP
.B listed
.B yes
to be found by name in the switchboard's directory; default
.BR no .
.TP
.B answer
.BR ask ,
.BR known ,
.B auto
or
.BR never :
the answer policy.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/longwire/profiles
The file.
.B LW_CONF
names its directory instead.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR longwire (1),
.BR longwire-switchboard (1),
.BR longwire-security (7)
